      academicExplanation: "The three essential elements of a contract are the form (Sighah, i.e. offer/Ijab and acceptance/Qabul), the contracting parties ('Aqidain), and the subject matter (Ma'qud 'alayh). Sanhuri's seven-component list adds unity of Majlis (contracting session), plurality of parties, sanity/capacity, deliverability of subject matter, a defined object (Mahall), and its beneficial/lawful nature. Offer (Ijab) is a firm proposal to create an obligation; acceptance (Qabul) must conform to the offer in all its details and, per the Hadith 'the contracting parties have the right of option until they separate,' must occur within the same Majlis (session) -- a requirement some scholars interpret liberally so that time reasonably taken to communicate acceptance still counts as the same 'meeting.' Offer and acceptance can be conveyed by words, gesture, or conduct; an offer lapses on withdrawal, death/incapacity of a party, termination of the Majlis without conclusion, destruction of the subject matter, or lapse of a stipulated acceptance period. The unity-of-session rule does not apply to agency, gift, or appointment of an executor for a minor's property. Regarding subject matter, it must be existing/existable, valuable, usable, capable of ownership and delivery, specified/quantified, and the seller must hold title and bear its risk; a nonexistent thing cannot be validly sold (hence short-selling is prohibited) except under the narrow, conditions-based exceptions of Salam and Istisna'a. The subject matter must also be intrinsically lawful (not wine, pork, intoxicants), free of legal charge (e.g. a mortgaged asset cannot be sold until redeemed), fit for the contract's purpose (public roads/parks cannot be sold), and not harmful. Precise determination can be by direct inspection or by sufficiently detailed description; if goods are present, majority view requires examination. The price/Thaman must be fixed and certain at contract time -- an uncertain or floating price (e.g. 'I will tell you the price later' or a price that varies by payment date without one final figure being fixed) voids the contract. Finally, possession (Qabza), physical or constructive (Hukmi), is required before on-selling -- 'he who buys foodstuff should not sell it until he has taken possession of it.'",
