      academicExplanation: "Contracts split by consideration/counter-value into Uqood-e-Mu'awadha (compensatory/commutative -- sale, hire, Wakalah, etc., where one party legitimately earns remuneration) and Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha/Tabarru' (noncommutative/gratuitous -- loan/Qard, gift/Hibah, guarantee/Kafalah, assignment of debt/Hawalah -- where no return/compensation is permissible). Within commutative contracts, sale (Bai') subdivides by object (Bai' Muqayadhah/barter, Bai' al-Hal/spot, Bai' al-Sarf/currency exchange, Bai' Salam, Bai' Mu'ajjal/deferred payment, Bai' Mutlaq/normal sale) and by price basis (Bai' Tawliyah/resale at cost, Bai' Murabaha/cost-plus, Bai' Wadhi'ah/resale at a loss -- together called Buyu' al-Amanat or 'trust sales' -- and Bai' Musawamah/bargained price with no cost reference). Ijarah splits into Ijarat al-Ashkhas (rendering services) and Ijarat al-Ashya (letting things); Istisna'a is manufacturing-on-order; Wakalah can be either commutative or noncommutative. Among Tabarru' contracts: Hibah, Wasiyyah, Waqf, Kafalah, 'Ariyah, Qard, and Hawalah -- of these, Kafalah, Qard, and Hawalah are directly relevant to Islamic banking, but banks cannot charge any profit against these contracts per se; they can only charge fees for genuinely separate services on a Wakalah or Ju'alah basis (e.g. amount-based, not time-based, fees for issuing L/Cs or guarantees). Legally, commutative contracts become void upon insertion of a void condition, whereas noncompensatory/Tabarru' contracts do not become void because of a void condition -- only the offending condition itself becomes ineffective (e.g. an interest-based loan: the interest condition is void, but the loan contract itself remains effective and the principal remains due). Similarly, Gharar does not invalidate noncompensatory contracts -- e.g. donation of a stray or unidentified animal is permissible even though its sale would not be valid.",
