      academicExplanation: "As a general rule, conditional contracts are not valid, though this needs nuance. Fiqh literature distinguishes three types of stipulation: T'aliq (conditions suspending a contract on a future event), Idafa (an extension delaying the start of a contract to a future time), and Iqtiran (concomitance -- a condition that varies the contract's terms). In all three, the contract may or may not be void even where the condition itself is void; jurists differ on the outcome. Hanafis and Hanbalis permit delay in beginning contracts like lease or agency (where property/usufruct transfers gradually over time), but not for sale. Regarding concomitant conditions, schools examine whether a condition agrees with or conflicts with the contract's purpose: stipulations that the buyer pay the price, or that the seller transfer full title, or fix payment in a certain currency, or require a pledge as security, are all valid; a condition barring the buyer from ever reselling is not approved. Hanbali jurists give the widest latitude, upholding every condition/stipulation not contradicting explicit Qur'an/Sunnah text; Hanafi, Shafi'i and Maliki jurists instead sort conditions into valid, irregular and void. Valid conditions confirm effects the Shari'ah already attributes to the act and are explicitly admitted by it -- e.g. Khiyar al-Shart (option of stipulation) and Khiyar al-Ru'yah (option of inspection), or a stipulation that goods won't be handed over until price is paid. A condition 'in aid of' the contract (e.g. requiring security/pledge for a credit price) is likewise valid, as is any condition that has become customary market practice, provided it doesn't contradict explicit Shari'ah text. A void condition is one that directly infringes a Shari'ah rule, harms one of the contracting parties, or derogates from completing the contract -- but per the general rule for Fasid contracts, a void condition inserted into an otherwise valid contract voids only the condition, leaving the contract itself valid and enforceable without regard to it. A condition of no advantage to either party is superfluous and unenforceable; a condition repugnant to the contract's purpose but advantageous to one party can make the transaction Fasid if inseparable from it.",
