  chapterSummary: "Chapter 5 lays out the Islamic law of contracts that underlies every Islamic banking product discussed later in the book. It starts from the classification of Mal, usufruct, and ownership, moves through the elements a valid 'Aqd requires (form, parties, subject matter), and the broad validity rules -- free consent, no Gharar, no Riba, no Qimar/Maisir, no prohibited contract-bundling, conformity with Maqasid al-Shari'ah, and profit tied to risk (Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm). It then examines Wa'dah (promise) and how modern scholarly consensus makes it binding in commercial reliance situations, together with the related devices of Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun. The Hanafi three-way classification of contracts as valid (Sahih), voidable (Fasid), or void (Batil) is explained in detail, along with the practically important distinction between commutative (Mu'awadha) and noncommutative (Tabarru') contracts, and finally the rules on conditional/contingent stipulations.",
